The Governor in Council (GIC) is a term used to refer to the Queen, acting on the advice of the Privy Council (collectively or on an individual basis), or, more commonly, the Governor General when the Queen is not personally present in Canada.

The GIC is an integral part of the executive branch of government and is therefore responsible for the issuance of Orders in Council. Orders in Council are legal instruments made by the GIC to carry out the executive branch's responsibilities. Orders in Council can be used to:

- Execute acts of Parliament;
- Appoint or dismiss officials or members of government bodies;
- Grant royal assent to bills passed by Parliament;
- Amend or repeal existing legislation;
- Grant pardons or commutations of sentences;
- Make other executive decisions.

The Governor General, as the representative of the Queen in Canada, plays a key role in the issuance of Orders in Council. The Governor General is responsible for:

- Receiving the advice of the Privy Council and the Cabinet;
- Signing Orders in Council on behalf of the Queen;
- Acting as the link between the executive and legislative branches of government.

The Privy Council, consisting of the Prime Minister and other senior government officials, provides advice to the Governor General and the Queen on the issuance of Orders in Council. The Cabinet, composed of the Prime Minister and other senior government officials, is responsible for the day-to-day management of government and provides advice to the Governor General and the Queen on the issuance of Orders in Council.

